@page
@model PrivacyModel
@{
    ViewData["Title"] = "Privacy";
}

<h1>@ViewData["Title"]</h1>

<p>
    Random GitHub is intentionally simple. It does not require an account
    and does not use advertising or third-party analytics.
</p>

<h2>GitHub personal access tokens</h2>

<p>
    You may optionally provide a fine-grained GitHub personal access token
    so Random GitHub can use your GitHub API allowance instead of the site's
    shared allowance.
</p>

<p>
    Your token is stored only in the server-side session. The browser receives
    a session cookie containing a session identifier, not the token itself.
    The token is sent only to GitHub's API and is removed when you choose
    <strong>Forget token</strong>, when the session expires, or when GitHub
    rejects the token.
</p>

<p>
    Random GitHub does not include your personal access token in logs or
    Mission Control telemetry.
</p>

<h2>Mission Control telemetry</h2>

<p>
    When a random repository is successfully selected, Random GitHub may send
    an event to my private Mission Control service for basic operational and
    usage telemetry.
</p>

<p>The event may include:</p>

<ul>
    <li>The selected repository ID and name</li>
    <li>Language, star count, and fork count</li>
    <li>Whether the repository is a fork</li>
    <li>Repository creation and last-push dates</li>
    <li>Whether the Skip forks option was enabled</li>
    <li>Whether a personal GitHub token was being used</li>
    <li>Whether the repository had a README</li>
    <li>How long the random selection took</li>
</ul>

<p>
    The Mission Control event does not include your GitHub token or your
    GitHub account identity.
</p>

<h2>Cookies</h2>

<p>
    Random GitHub uses a session cookie when needed for features such as the
    optional personal GitHub token. It is not used for advertising or
    cross-site tracking.
</p>

<h2>External services</h2>

<p>
    Random GitHub communicates with GitHub's API to retrieve public repository
    information and rendered README content. Repository READMEs may also contain
    images hosted by GitHub or other external sites, which your browser may
    request when displaying the README.
</p>

<p>
    Hosting, reverse-proxy, and network infrastructure may also process normal
    request information such as IP addresses as part of serving the site.
</p>